.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMM-EXPORT 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emm-export \- export one or more modules' MongoDB data to a local JSON file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B emm export
(\fB\-\-module\fR|\fB\-m\fR \fIMODULE\fR[,\fIMODULE\fR...] | \fB\-\-all\fR)
[\fB\-\-full\fR]
[\fB\-\-file\fR|\fB\-f\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-passphrase\fR [\fIPHRASE\fR]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Exports one or more modules' own MongoDB collections to a single local
JSON file - a lightweight backup/inspection tool, not a full
.BR mongodump (1)
replacement (no resume, no incremental export, and no restore
counterpart yet). The caller must be authenticated as an administrator;
see
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1)
for how that's checked.
.PP
.B \-\-module
takes a comma-separated list of modules
.RI ( "e.g. " esm,eqs,ens ),
so a single run can cover several modules at once, each contributing
its own collections to the one output file.
.B \-\-all
exports every module instead. The two are mutually exclusive - exactly
one is required.
.PP
For each exported module, by default only its top-level resource
collection(s) are included - the same resources euclid-cli's own
.I list-*
actions show: queues
.RI ( eqs ),
topics and their subscriptions
.RI ( ens ),
buckets and their subscriptions
.RI ( esm ),
users/groups/accounts/namespaces
.RI ( eam ),
or the module registry
.RI ( emm ).
.B \-\-full
additionally includes the bulk child data each resource owns - EQS/ENS
messages and ESM objects - which is opt-in because it can dwarf the
container records around it.
.PP
An export holds everything those collections hold - access-key secrets
among it, and for
.I ekm
the key material itself - and then lives wherever it was written, on a
laptop or a backup share, with none of the protection the database it
came from has.
.B \-\-passphrase
seals it: the contents are encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a key derived
from the passphrase (PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256, 600000 iterations, a fresh salt
per archive), and only the envelope - which modules, when, and how to
derive the key - stays readable. Exporting
.I ekm
requires one.
.PP
There is no recovery. Nothing keeps a copy of the passphrase, and an
archive whose passphrase is lost is lost with it - which is why giving
.B \-\-passphrase
without a value, and being asked for it twice, is the better way to use
it.
.PP
Each document is rendered using MongoDB's relaxed Extended JSON (ISO
date strings, plain numbers,
.I {"$oid": ...}
for ObjectIds), so the file both reads naturally and preserves BSON
types that plain JSON can't represent unambiguously.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR[,\fIMODULE\fR...]
Comma-separated modules to export, from
.IR eam ", " emm ", " emo ", " ens ", " eqs ", "
and
.IR esm .
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-all ;
exactly one of the two is required.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Exports every module. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-module .
.TP
.B \-\-full
Also export the bulk child data collection(s) (EQS/ENS messages, ESM
objects), not just the top-level resources. Ignored for modules with no
child data
.RI ( eam ", " emm ", " emo ).
Defaults to off.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Output file path. Defaults to
.IR MODULES\-export\-UNIXTIME.json
in the current directory, where
.I MODULES
is the requested module names joined with
.RI ( - )
(or
.I all
when
.B \-\-all
was given).
.TP
.B \-\-passphrase " " [\fIPHRASE\fR]
Encrypt the archive under this passphrase.
.IP
Given without a value, the passphrase is asked for on the terminal
instead - twice, and without echoing - which is what keeps it out of the
shell's history and out of every
.BR ps (1)
listing for as long as the export runs. Reading it from a pipe works too,
for a script that has it somewhere safer than its own command line.
.SH OUTPUT
The written file is a single JSON object:
.RS
.TP
.I modules
Array of the exported module names.
.TP
.I full
Whether the bulk child collection(s) were included.
.TP
.I exportedAt
ISO 8601 timestamp of when the export ran.
.TP
.I collections
Object keyed by MongoDB collection name (e.g.
.IR eqs_queue ", " eqs_message ),
each value a JSON array of that collection's documents. Collections
from every requested module are merged into this one object (their
names already carry the module prefix, so there's no collision).
.RE
.PP
A sealed archive carries the same
.IR modules ", " full " and " exportedAt
in the clear, plus
.IR encrypted ", " cipher ", " kdf ", " iterations " and " salt
- everything needed to ask for the right passphrase and derive its key -
and instead of
.I collections
an array of
.IR frames ,
each an encrypted export payload. Several modules are exported one frame
at a time under a shared key, so no single frame has to hold an entire
installation.
.RE
.PP
On success, a small JSON summary
.RI ( modules ", " full ", " file )
is printed to standard output - not the exported data itself, which
only goes to the file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The export was written to
.IR PATH .
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments (including giving both or neither of
.BR \-\-module " and " \-\-all ),
an unknown module name, the stored session is missing or not an
administrator's, the request could not be sent, the server rejected it,
or the output file could not be opened for writing. A passphrase that was
asked for and left empty, or whose confirmation did not match, also stops
here - before anything is exported, since the alternative is a file
nobody can open.
.SH EXAMPLES
Export just the queues known to EQS:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm export --module eqs
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export ESM buckets together with every object they contain, to a chosen
file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm export \e
    --module esm --full --file esm-backup.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export several modules in one file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm export --module esm,eqs,ens
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export every module, including bulk child data:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm export --all --full
.fi
.RE
.PP
Seal an export, being asked for the passphrase rather than typing it into
the command line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm export \e
    --all --full --passphrase --file euclid-backup.json
.fi
.RE
